	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Label="Compat">
		<PackageVersion Include="System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager"             Version="$(Net8LatestForNuget)"        Condition="!$([MSBuild]::IsTargetFrameworkCompatible('$(TargetFramework)', 'net9.0'))" />
		<PackageVersion Include="System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager"             Version="$(Net9LatestForNuget)"        Condition="'$(TargetFramework)'=='net9.0'"  />
		<PackageVersion Include="System.Configuration.ConfigurationManager"             Version="$(Net10LatestForNuget)"       Condition="'$(TargetFramework)'=='net10.0'" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Label="Manage Transient Packages with Vulnerabilities">
		<!--suppress invalid System.Private.Uri errors : https://github.com/dotnet/sdk/issues/42651-->
		<NuGetAuditSuppress
			Include="
			https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-5f2m-466j-3848;
			https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-x5qj-9vmx-7g6g;
			https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xhfc-gr8f-ffwc" />

		<!--
		CVE-2025-6965 / GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q: SQLite before 3.50.2. The transitive native package
		SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 arrives at 2.1.11 (vulnerable) via Microsoft.Data.Sqlite /
		Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite. Patched releases DO now exist (2.1.12, 3.50.3, 3.53.3 —
		the project renumbered to track SQLite versions), but Microsoft.Data.Sqlite still resolves
		2.1.11, so the vulnerable package stays in a consumer's graph and tools such as LINQPad report
		it. At runtime the patched binary wins wherever SourceGear.sqlite3 is present: tests reference
		it (>= 3.50.2, currently 3.53.4), and the LINQPad 5 lpx overwrites the native with it in
		LinqToDB.LINQPad.csproj's PostBuild2 target.
		CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled is false here (see the Roslyn note above), so pinning
		requires an explicit PackageReference on SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3 in each consuming project
		rather than a PackageVersion entry alone.
		The LINQPad driver nuget is the exception and has no such PackageReference: it declares its
		database clients per connection instead (IDatabaseProvider.GetNuGetPackages), so the pin lives
		in SQLiteProvider.GetNuGetPackages, which returns this version next to Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.
		SourceGear.sqlite3 is provisioned next to it for the SQLiteClassic variant only - the two packages
		place e_sqlite3 at conflicting paths (see SQLite.Runtime.props) - so the Microsoft.Data.Sqlite
		variant relies on this pin alone. That separation is per-provider: the two paths that cannot tell
		which client a connection needs - DatabaseProviders.GetAllNuGetPackages, and the static-context
		branch of DriverHelper.OverrideDriverDependencies - still hand LINQPad both variants together, as
		the nuspec did before. Whether LINQPad's per-connection restore honours it over the
		transitive 2.1.11 is unconfirmed against a real install; check it in the LINQPad pass of the
		release test matrix.
		Keep this entry and the suppression below even though no project references the id: the version
		is read at build time by the generated NuGetPackageVersions class.
		-->
		<PackageVersion Include="SQLitePCLRaw.lib.e_sqlite3" Version="3.53.3" />
		<NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q" />

